\begin{align*} 8 \times h = 40 \iff h = 5 \end{align*}
Let the intersection of $MN$ with $AB$ be $P$. So
\begin{align*} |PP| = |PN| = \frac{h}{2} = 2.5. \end{align*}
Since $ABCD$ is a rectangle, the length of $\overline{AB}$ is $8$, while the length of $\overline{AD}$ is $5$. Since we can see that $\overline{MC} = \overline{CN}$, we conclude that the area of rectangle $NMBP$ equals the area of rectangle $BCMN$, which we denote below with $X.$ Using the formula for the area of a rectangle, we determine
\begin{align*} X = |MP| \cdot |MN| = |BN| \cdot |BC|. \end{align*}
By take on one measurement and solve the other:
\begin{align*} X = |MP| \cdot |MN| = |BN| \cdot |BC| = |BC| \cdot \left ( \frac{|BC|}{2} \right ) = \frac{|BC|^2}{2}. \end{align*}
Next consider the trapezoid $MAFN$, the sum of whose area and the area $X$ is equal to $ABCD$.
\begin{align*} X + \text{area of trapezoid } MAFN &= \text{area of rectangle } ABCD \\ X + \text{area of trapezoid } MAFN &= 40 = |AB| \cdot |AD| \\ X + \frac{(|AB| + |MF|) \cdot (|AN| - |MF|)}{2} &= 40 \\ X + \frac{15 \cdot \left ( \left ( \frac{15}{2} \right ) - \left ( \frac{2}{5} \right ) + 5 - \left ( \frac{2}{5} \right ) + 2 - \left ( \frac{2}{5} \right ) + \frac{3}{5} - 1 \right )}{2} &= 40 \\ X + \frac{15 \cdot \left ( \frac{79}{10} - \frac{24}{5} \right )}{2} &= 40 \\ X + \frac{15 \cdot \left ( \frac{31}{10} \right )}{2} &= 40 \\ X + \frac{465}{20} &= 40 \\ X &= 40 - \frac{93}{4} \\ X &= 17 \frac{1}{4}. \end{align*}
Thus $\boxed{ \text{answer} \quad 17 \frac{1}{4} }$.
