We need to solve a system of congruences:
\begin{align}
20n &\equiv 4 \pmod{26}.
\end{align}
Since $\gcd(20, 26)$ does not divide $4$, we need a common factor:
\begin{align}
a \cdot 20 &\equiv 4 \pmod{26 \cdot a}.
\end{align}
Computing the Extended Euclidean Algorithm for:
\begin{align}
20ax + 26 \cdot 26ay &= 4,
\end{align}
gives
\begin{align}
ay &= 2.
\end{align}
From which, we have $y = 13$, thus
\begin{align}
13 \cdot \frac{20}{26} \equiv 1 \pmod{26}.
\end{align}
Multiplying the congruence by $13$, we get
\begin{align}
260x &\equiv 52 \pmod{676}.
\end{align}
Therefore,
\begin{align}
20 \cdot 13n &\equiv 52 \pmod{26 \cdot 26}.
\end{align}
Hence, the maximum possible number of people that could be in the Trumpington marching band is $52$?
